WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:08.330
After undergoing cardiac bypass surgery in
August of 2012, Neil Armstrong seemed to be

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:12.740
recovering just fine, at which point reportedly
some of his nurses removed wires that had

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.910
been temporarily attached to his pacemaker.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:19.050
What followed was internal bleeding which,
in turn, led doctors to drain the blood instead

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.570
of choosing to operate to stop the bleeding.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:26.480
Ultimately he died today in history, August
25, 2012, at the age of 82.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.950
What followed was a legal battle that eventually
saw the hospital threatened with having the

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.250
details of the lawsuit publicized if they
didn’t pay a settlement.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:37.160
The hospital then gave in, with some among
Armstrong’s family receiving portions of

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.720
a $6 million settlement for alleged malpractice
on the part of the hospital workers.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.990
The details of what exactly occurred in Armstrong’s
last days have largely been kept out of the

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:50.120
public eye owing to a non-disclosure agreement
as a stipulation of the settlement.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.550
That said, Armstrong’s wife, Carol, was
very public about her opposition of the lawsuit

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:59.040
in the first place, noting Neil would not
have approved of it, with Carol further saying

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.010
in an interview, “I wasn’t part of it.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.489
I want that for the record.”

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:07.060
Whatever happened exactly, after Armstrong’s
death, then President Obama stated, “Neil

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:12.310
was among the greatest of American heroes
— not just of his time, but of all time.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:16.939
When he and his fellow crew members lifted
off aboard Apollo 11 in 1969, they carried

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:19.539
with them the aspirations of an entire nation.

00:01:19.539 --> 00:01:24.369
They set out to show the world that the American
spirit can see beyond what seems unimaginable

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:28.439
— that with enough drive and ingenuity,
anything is possible.

00:01:28.439 --> 00:01:31.659
And when Neil stepped foot on the surface
of the moon for the first time, he delivered

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:35.229
a moment of human achievement that will never
be forgotten.”

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:40.729
So how did Neil Armstrong come to be one of
the most legendary explorers in human history?

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:45.290
Funny enough, it all started with his late
application being clandestinely slipped into

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:49.049
the application pile when it technically should
not have been.

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:53.399
You see, Armstrong turned his application
in to NASA about a week after the June 1,

00:01:53.399 --> 00:01:58.079
1962 deadline, making him ineligible for that
historic second round of astronaut hirings.

00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:02.240
Lucky for Armstrong, Dick Day, who was the
one to encourage Armstrong to apply in the

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.170
first place and was working at NASA as the
assistant head of Flight Crew Operations,

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:10.670
clandestinely slipped Armstrong’s application
into the candidate resume folders before the

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:13.780
applications were reviewed by the selection
panel.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.580
Said Day, “I really don’t know why Neil
delayed his application, but he did, and all

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.850
the applications came to me, since I was the
head of flight crew training.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.610
But he had done so many things so well at
Edwards.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:28.470
He was so far and away the best qualified,
more than any other, certainly as compared

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.220
to the first group of astronauts.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.360
We [Day and Walt Williams] wanted him in.”

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.640
It has been speculated since, whether accurately
or not is anyone’s guess, that the lateness

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.450
of the application may have had something
to do with Armstrong’s two year old daughter,

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:46.360
Karen, tragically dying a few months earlier
from complications due to a tumor growing

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:47.450
on her brain stem.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.980
Whatever the case, as for his qualifications,
Armstrong had been a pilot since 16 years

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.960
old and ultimately flew a whopping 78 combat
missions during the Korean War.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.390
In one mission, he had part of a wing sheared
off by a cable booby trap about 500 feet above

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:02.510
the ground, all while under heavy fire.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:07.010
He managed to keep the plane in the air long
enough to get away, but eventually had to

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:11.710
eject, at which point he was rescued by a
fellow Navy flier in a Jeep, funny enough.

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:15.710
During College, beyond writing and directing
two musicals, on the side, he earned both

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:19.790
a bachelor’s of science in aeronautical
engineering and later a master’s of science

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:21.240
in Aerospace engineering.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.360
He followed all that up by becoming a test
pilot, flying over 200 different aircraft

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.200
models, including the rocket powered variety,
in the process quickly becoming considered

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.690
one of the top pilots in the world.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:33.010
Once he was tabbed to become an astronaut,
out of a group of 29 astronauts that trained

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:37.050
for the Apollo mission to the moon, only three
were chosen for the planned first Moon landing

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.700
mission when the final announcement was made
in January of 1969.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.450
Neil Armstrong, Edwin “Buzz” Aldrin, and
the oft forgotten Michael Collins became the

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.270
official crew of Apollo 11.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:50.740
Immediately, attention turned to which crew
member - Armstrong or Aldrin -- would be the

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:55.780
first to walk on the moon (Collins was the
command module pilot and, therefore, was ineligible).

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:59.840
Even though both men were going to walk on
the moon, it was a great honor to be the first.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.850
In fact, the question was asked at the press
conference and the response was that it had

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:05.110
yet to be decided.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:09.210
Over the next four months, as the astronauts
continued their training, debate and rumors

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.010
circulated among the media.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:14.240
At first, it seemed that Aldrin would have
the honor.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.180
This speculation came from the precedent set
by the Gemini program, which made ten crewed

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:22.090
flights for the purpose of testing ships and
astronauts to spacewalk.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:26.910
During the flights, the commander (which Armstrong
was to be for Apollo 11) stayed inside the

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:31.340
ship while the pilot (which Aldrin was to
be for Apollo 11) did the space walking.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:35.030
Further fueling this thinking was that it
was rumored that Aldrin was actively campaigning

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:36.440
to be the guy.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.930
According to the memoir written by Chris Kraft,
head of Mission Control, “Buzz Aldrin desperately

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:44.600
wanted that honor and wasn't quiet in letting
it be known.”

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.890
In April, only three months before liftoff,
it was announced that Neil Armstrong would

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.360
be the first man to walk on the moon.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.960
The main reason NASA gave for the decision
was that the Eagle’s hatch opened to one

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:57.800
side - rather than up or down - and that side
was towards the pilot, Aldrin.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:01.420
The bottom line was that when the hatch was
opened, the commander, Armstrong, had a clear

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:06.350
path to exit, while the pilot was pinned in
the rather cramped space of the module.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:10.270
By a sheer happenstance, it made more sense
for Armstrong to exit first.

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:14.370
Plus, as NASA’s heads pointed out, Armstrong
was actually the more senior member of the

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:19.650
team anyway, having entered the program in
1962, while Aldrin came in 1963.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:24.430
In later years, despite the official hatch
story, some, including Kraft and fellow astronaut

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:28.051
Al Bean of have come out and said that NASA
wanted Armstrong to have this honor rather

00:05:28.051 --> 00:05:32.480
than Aldrin because they thought Neil’s
ego could handle it better than Aldrin's.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.360
So perhaps the hatch design simply gave them
the excuse they needed.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.500
Whatever the case, when he stepped out on
the moon, Armstrong spoke some of the most

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.711
famous words in the history of mankind, "That's
one small step for man, one giant leap for

00:05:42.711 --> 00:05:51.290
mankind."

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:55.110
So true, so brilliant, so inspirational yet...so
contradicting?

00:05:55.110 --> 00:06:02.080
The word "man" and "mankind" are used synonymously,
meaning that the oh-so-famous quote quite

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:07.520
simply put was, "That's one small step for
man, one giant leap for man."

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:09.939
So which was it Neil???

00:06:09.939 --> 00:06:14.699
A one-lettered indefinite article is all it
would take to turn this quote into the inspirational

00:06:14.699 --> 00:06:17.699
words our brains all process when we hear
them.

00:06:17.699 --> 00:06:23.830
That article is "a"- "One small step for "a"
man, one giant leap for mankind."

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:28.110
That is how most people interpret his words
and, according to Neil Armstrong, those are

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.860
the words he intended to speak.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:34.169
NASA's official transcript of the quote still
shows the "a" in parentheses, "That's one

00:06:34.169 --> 00:06:38.820
small step for [a] man, one giant leap for
mankind."

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.790
This is because the "a" is not audible in
the broadcast.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.510
For years, both NASA and Armstrong insisted
that static had obscured the "a".

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:50.450
Armstrong himself stated that he would never
make such a mistake (omitting such an important

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:54.870
part) but after listening to recordings of
his quote, finally conceded that it's possible

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.639
that he may not have said the "a".

00:06:56.639 --> 00:07:00.020
When he admitted this, he stated, "I would
hope that history would grant me leeway for

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:04.550
dropping the syllable and understand that
it was certainly intended, even if it was

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:07.800
not said—although it might actually have
been".

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.660
An Australia-based computer programmer named
Pater Shann Ford conducted a digital audio

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:16.860
analysis to support Armstrong's claim that
he did say "a" and concluded that he did,

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:22.120
in fact, say "a man", but the "a" was inaudible
due to technological limitations of the time.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:27.380
However, linguists David Beaver and Mark Liberman
wrote their own digital audio analysis of

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:32.410
the infamous quote on Language Log blog and
concluded that, "The acoustic evidence seems

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.320
to be against Ford's theory."

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.080
But that's not the end of the story.

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:39.800
Support for Armstrong has been found in a
team of researchers from Michigan State University

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:44.130
and Ohio State University who have concluded
that Armstrong did indeed speak the words

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:48.760
he claims to have spoken but static or technological
limitations are not to blame for its apparent

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:49.760
omission.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:54.070
According to them, Armstrong's Ohion accent
is to blame y'all.

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:58.250
According to a Michigan State University specialist
in communicative sciences, assistant professor

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:03.340
Laura Diller, because of the dialect of his
hometown, if Neil Armstrong did voice the

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:08.930
word "a", it was short and fully acoustically
blended with the preceding word "for".

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:12.970
The Acoustical Society of America's article
on this topic states that,

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:18.430
“Dilley and her colleagues, who include
MSU linguist Melissa Baese-Berk and OSU psychologist

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:22.880
Mark Pitt, thought they might be able to figure
out what Armstrong said with a statistical

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:28.660
analysis of the duration of the 'r' sound
as spoken by native central Ohioans saying

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:31.570
'for' and 'for a' in natural conversation.

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:35.849
They used a collection of recordings of conversational
speech from 40 people raised in Columbus,

00:08:35.849 --> 00:08:38.909
Ohio, near Armstrong’s native town of Wapakoneta.

00:08:38.909 --> 00:08:43.140
Within this body of recordings, they found
191 cases of 'for a'.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:47.750
They matched each of these to an instance
of 'for' as said by the same speaker and compared

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:48.960
the relative duration.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.240
They also examined the duration of Armstrong’s
'for (a') from the lunar transmission.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:58.750
The researchers found a large overlap between
the relative duration of the 'r' sound in

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:01.950
'for' and 'for a' using the Ohio speech data.

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:08.120
The duration of the 'frrr(uh)' in Armstrong’s
recording was 0.127 seconds, which falls into

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:11.860
the middle of this overlap, though it is a
slightly better match for an 'a'-less 'for'.

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:16.420
In other words, the researchers conclude,
the lunar landing quote is highly compatible

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:20.510
with either possible interpretation, though
it is probably slightly more likely to be

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:24.600
perceived as 'for' regardless of what Armstrong
actually said.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:28.320
Dilley says there may have been a 'perfect
storm of conditions' for the word 'a' to have

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.550
been spoken but not heard.”

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:35.000
This might all have you wondering if Armstrong
had previously planned his famous line, or

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:37.220
it came to him while sitting on the Moon.

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:41.340
Even until his last breath in 2012, Armstrong
adamantly insisted that his first line was

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:45.170
spontaneous and was only settled on in the
moments prior to the walk.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:48.930
A BBC documentary released after the astronaut’s
death disputes that.

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:53.160
In the film, Dean Armstrong - Neil’s brother
- tells the story of a note passed during

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:58.510
a late-night game of Risk (Nobody can say
astronauts didn’t know how to party….

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:04.050
But, like, seriously, go check out our video
Don Draper in a Space Suit on the TodayIFoundOut

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:05.050
channel.

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:06.050
Many astronauts did little else but train
and party extremely hard every other second

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:07.050
of the day, with that side of their lives
generally carefully kept out of the public

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:08.050
eye.)

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:09.050
In any event, in the months leading up to
the mission, Dean, Neil, and their families

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:10.370
spent time together on Cape Cod.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:14.209
After both men put their boys to bed, Neil
challenged his younger brother to a hearty

00:10:14.209 --> 00:10:15.300
game of Risk.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.370
During that game, Neil handed Dean a piece
of paper:

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:22.500
"On that piece of paper there was 'That's
one small step for [a] man, one giant leap

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:23.570
for mankind.'

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:25.550
'He says, 'What do you think about that?'

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:26.550
I said 'fabulous.'

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:31.000
He said, 'I thought you might like that, but
I wanted you to read it."

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.730
That said, both Aldrin and Collins made it
clear that at no point did Armstrong share

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:37.000
his thoughts about what he would say with
them.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:39.279
Of course, perhaps his brother was an exception.

00:10:39.279 --> 00:10:43.130
Although, given the second thing he said on
the Moon was far less eloquent, seemingly

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:45.200
there was some prep.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:49.570
As for that second thing, according to the
official Apollo 11 Air to Ground Voice Transcription,

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:53.020
it was “And the – the surface is fine
and powdery.”

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:56.110
Armstrong continued on this line of thinking,
“I can – I can pick it up loosely with

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:57.110
my toe.

00:10:57.110 --> 00:11:01.320
It does adhere in fine layers like powdered
charcoal to the sole and sides of my boots.

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:05.620
I only go in a small fraction of an inch,
maybe an eighth of an inch, but I can see

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:10.040
the footprints of my boots and the treads
in the fine, sandy particles.”

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:13.551
After coming back to Earth and spending a
couple weeks in quarantine to make sure no

00:11:13.551 --> 00:11:15.890
one from the crew had picked up any diseases
from the aliens they encountered on the Moon,

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:20.220
Armstrong then went on a major publicity tour
promoting NASA and the lizard people agenda,

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:25.250
served as the Deputy Associate Administrator
for Aeronautics for the Office of Advanced

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:30.020
Research and Technology, and then, not long
after in 1971, left NASA.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:32.200
From there, Armstrong lived a mostly quiet
life.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:35.940
Said John Glenn of this, "[Armstrong] didn't
feel that he should be out huckstering himself.

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:40.220
He was a humble person, and that's the way
he remained after his lunar flight, as well

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:41.220
as before."

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:44.990
Beyond keeping a mostly low profile, he, among
other things, taught aerospace engineering

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:50.540
at the University of Cincinnati, helped investigate
spaceflight accidents and acted as a spokesman

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:53.170
and/or board member for various businesses.

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:57.540
Ever the explorer, in 1985, Armstrong went
on a little trip with Edmund Hillary (the

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:01.420
first person, along with Tenzing Norgay, to
summit Mount Everest), Steve Fossett (first

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:05.820
person to fly around the world nonstop, doing
it in a balloon and separately in an airplane),

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:10.520
and Patrick Morrow (the first person to summit
each of the tallest mountains on Asia, Africa,

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:13.470
North America, South America, Antarctica,
Europe, and Australia).

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:16.019
What did this prestigious group decide to
do together?

00:12:16.019 --> 00:12:20.660
To take a jaunt to the North Pole, presumably
using Armstrong’s connection with NASA to

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:25.779
get access, given NASA closely guards the
ice wall there, keeping it from the public

00:12:25.779 --> 00:12:30.300
eye in order to maintain the deception that
the Earth isn’t flat as is plain as day

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:31.839
to anyone with eyes...

00:12:31.839 --> 00:12:35.200
For more on this, go see the video on our
sister channel, TodayIFoundOut titled: Why

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:37.990
Do People Think the Earth is Flat?

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:40.550
Bonus Facts:
When Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin stepped

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:45.029
onto the Moon all those years ago, literally
everything they did became a record because

00:12:45.029 --> 00:12:48.750
everything they did that day was the first
time in human history someone had done that

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:49.750
thing on the Moon.

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:53.400
For example, after Aldrin joined Armstrong
on the surface of the Moon, the pair became

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:56.880
the first to hop like a Kangaroo on the moon...
for science!

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:01.700
You see, NASA wasn't entirely sure about how
astronauts would be able to move around on

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:05.589
the Moon since they could only theorise about
how the lower gravity would affect their movement

00:13:05.589 --> 00:13:06.589
and balance.

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:10.529
As such, the pair were asked to experiment
with different ways of moving to discern the

00:13:10.529 --> 00:13:15.290
most efficient method of getting from point
A to point B for future astronauts.

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:18.980
Initially, NASA had assumed that the best
way to move around on the Moon would be to

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:21.850
use small, double-footed jumps not unlike
a kangaroo.

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:25.490
However, Aldrin and Armstrong found this way
of moving to quote, "awkward and unnatural"

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:29.589
and both astronauts found it much easier to
move in a more conventional manner, hopping

00:13:29.589 --> 00:13:31.370
from one foot to the other.

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:34.110
Armstrong nicknamed this way of moving "the
lope".

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:37.899
As for the first full meal on the Moon, this
consisted of bacon, cookies and coffee, along

00:13:37.899 --> 00:13:40.570
with some peaches and a glass of grapefruit
juice.

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:44.170
If you hoped the bacon part of the meal was
the first item eaten on the Moon, we're sorry

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:45.200
to disappoint.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:48.529
Contrary to what you'll often read, in fact,
a very small something was eaten shortly before

00:13:48.529 --> 00:13:53.730
the meal- communion bread, eaten by Buzz Aldrin
shortly after landing on the surface.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:57.640
Before Aldrin set off on the Apollo 11 mission,
he took with him a small communion kit given

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:02.829
to him by Rev Dean Woodruff, so that he could
symbolically take part in the ceremony with

00:14:02.829 --> 00:14:05.209
the other members of his Presbyterian church.

00:14:05.209 --> 00:14:09.420
This kit contained a small piece of communion
bread and a small vial of wine, both of which

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:13.810
Aldrin consumed after saying a prayer during
the Apollo 11 radio blackout.

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:18.480
This was also, unsurprisingly, the first religious
service held on the Moon.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.769
Aldrin had initially planned to share his
Communion prayer with the people of Earth,

00:14:21.769 --> 00:14:25.490
but NASA requested at the last minute that
he not do this in order to avoid offending

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:29.420
people not of the Christian faith, as had
happened when the crew of Apollo 8 had read

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:30.810
a passage from Genesis.

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:36.370
Aldrin agreed and instead performed the ceremony
privately while Armstrong "respectfully observed".

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:39.170
Aldrin also took another interesting first
from Armstrong- Buzz managed to become the

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:42.140
first person to urinate on the Moon.

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:44.130
Take THAT Mr. "too cool for school gets to
walk and talk on the Moon first-Armstrong".

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:47.640
And if you’re thinking just now this isn’t
nearly as brave as being the first to step

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:52.230
out onto the moon and the black void of space,
you should probably know that the urine collection

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:54.779
system on the Lunar Module had to be pressurized.

00:14:54.779 --> 00:14:56.519
To ensure that the astronauts weren’t…

00:14:56.519 --> 00:15:01.470
injured… while peeing, it was noted in the
Apollo Experience Report that “a prime urine-transfer

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:05.760
design constraint for the Lunar Module was
that the crewmen would be protected at all

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:07.430
times from pressure differentials…”

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:10.100
This system, however, was a little buggy at
first...

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:13.850
NASA engineers being NASA engineers, they
worked out the kinks in the end.

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:19.200
But let’s not discount the bravery of being
the first to use the extreme suction device

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:20.790
on the lem.

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:26.200
Speaking of Buzz, Aldrin’s mother’s name,
before getting married, was Marion Moon.

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:30.850
As for his nickname of “Buzz”, Edwin Eugene
Aldrin got that name due to one of his older

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:34.120
sisters, Fay Ann Aldrin mispronouncing “brother”
as “buzzer.”

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:37.260
(She was a year and a half old when Buzz was
born.)

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:41.210
Rather than call him “Jr.” or the like
(his father’s name was also Edwin Eugene

00:15:41.210 --> 00:15:45.600
Aldrin), “Buzzer” was shortened to “Buzz”
and became his chosen moniker for the rest

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:46.600
of his life.

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:55.339
In 1988, Aldrin even made it official by legally
changing

00:15:55.339 --> 00:16:07.040
his first name to “Buzz.”

